The Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL) NanoElectronicMOdeling-3D package.
Copyright (C) 2002 California Institute of Technology (Caltech)


General Information
===================

NanoElectronic MOdeling (NEMO) 3D is a simulation tool designed 
to analyze the electronic properties of zincblende nanostructures within 
a 10 or 20 orbital tight-binding description.  This atomistic approach 
enables an accurate characterization of the electronic structure for 
nanostructures whose minimum feature sizes are less than a few tens of 
monolayers, where traditional envelope function approximations would 
ordinarily fail.  Appropriate tight-binding parameterization must, 
of course, be used.  Parameterizations of several materials, including 
GaAs, InAs, Si, and Ge, are included with this distribution.  The status 
of this code is very alpha; only recently has any progress been made 
to advance its status from that of research code to one which may be 
used by those without intimate knowledge of the code itself.  For 
instance, at present there is no GUI aside from a basic editor for the 
input card, and some features are not yet fully implemented.  Furthermore,
the software has been tested on only a limited set of platforms
(gnu/linux on x86, and OS X on powerpc).  However, NEMO3D is under active 
developement, regarding both its user interface and the underlying physics.  
For more detailed information, please visit the NEMO-3D homepage at:

http://hpc.jpl.nasa.gov/PEP/gekco/nemo3D/index.html



